      "sketch": "Replace the class with a dataclass:\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Customer:\n    customer_id: int\n    name: str\n    email: str\n    tier: str = \"standard\"\n\n    def is_premium(self) -> bool:\n        return self.tier == \"premium\"\n```\n\nAll attributes become public, eliminating the getter methods. The `is_premium` method remains to preserve behaviour. This removes the private attributes, reduces the method count to one, and collapses the method‑attribute graph into a single component.",
      "risk_notes": "Existing code that calls `customer.customer_id()`, `customer.name()`, etc. will break; callers must be updated to access attributes directly. If the domain requires strict encapsulation for future validation or lazy loading, this change may be too aggressive. The `is_premium` method remains, so behaviour is largely preserved."
